<%!-- ============================================================
  Docs / Host your own pack registry — private pack distribution on
  customer infrastructure, built with the same tool (packctl) that
  publishes the public registry. Distribution ≠ trust, unless a deployment
  configures this registry as its catalog.
============================================================ --%>

<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="pack-registry"
      updated="August 18, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"author", "Author & validate"},
        {"get-packctl", "Get packctl"},
        {"build", "Build the tree"},
        {"host", "Host it anywhere"},
        {"install", "Install fleet-wide"},
        {"publish", "Publish safely"},
        {"tradeoffs", "What changes"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="pack-registry">
        <:dek>
          An emisar pack registry is a <strong class="text-zinc-200">static file tree over
            HTTPS</strong>. You can host it in a bucket, nginx, MinIO, or an artifact store. Build
          the tree with <code>packctl</code>. Install each pack from its immutable URL and pin its
          reviewed hash.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Registry paths become v1 compatibility surfaces. Read
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/compatibility"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Compatibility and deprecation</.link>
        before you publish a stable internal endpoint.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Distribution and trust are separate. The runner checks the supplied <code>sha256</code>
        hash during installation. It checks the pack hash again when it loads the pack. The portal
        trusts only catalog entries from its configured catalog. Other packs remain pending until
        an admin trusts their exact version and hash.
      </p>
      <.docs_callout kind={:warn} title="The configured catalog publisher is a trust authority">
        A valid catalog can authorize a matching pack already installed on a runner. Its
        publisher also chooses each pack's risk, kind, and retirement floor. It cannot install
        packs or bypass
        descriptor checks, argument validation, or local admission. emisar does not verify a
        separate catalog signature. Protect publishing access and replace a suspected catalog
        before another install.
      </.docs_callout>

      <.agent_skill_cta
        class="mt-8"
        skill="author-pack"
        prompt="Author an emisar action pack, publish it to my own pack registry, and install it hash-pinned on a runner. Use the author-pack skill: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/AndrewDryga/emisar/main/skills/author-pack/SKILL.md"
      >
        The <code class="text-zinc-300">author-pack</code>
        skill walks your agent through authoring, publishing, and installing from your own registry.
      </.agent_skill_cta>

      <%!-- Steps --%>
      <ol class="mt-12 space-y-8">
        <li id="author" class="scroll-mt-24 rounded-2xl border border-zinc-900 bg-zinc-950/60 p-6">
          <header class="flex items-center gap-3">
            <span class="grid h-8 w-8 shrink-0 place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 font-mono text-sm font-bold text-brand-300 ring-1 ring-brand-500/30">
              1
            </span>
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-zinc-50">Author and validate your packs</h2>
          </header>
          <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            Packs are directories of YAML. The
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/publishing-packs"}
              class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
            >
              Author your own pack
            </.link>
            walks the format, and the
            <.link navigate={~p"/docs/action-packs"} class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">
              Pack reference
            </.link>
            covers every field. Validation runs the same loader and hash path the runner enforces:
          </p>
          <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell" copy_text="emisar pack validate ./billing-tools">emisar pack validate ./billing-tools</.docs_code>
        </li>

        <li
          id="get-packctl"
          class="scroll-mt-24 rounded-2xl border border-zinc-900 bg-zinc-950/60 p-6"
        >
          <header class="flex items-center gap-3">
            <span class="grid h-8 w-8 shrink-0 place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 font-mono text-sm font-bold text-brand-300 ring-1 ring-brand-500/30">
              2
            </span>
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-zinc-50">Get packctl</h2>
          </header>
          <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <code>packctl</code>
            builds and publishes registries. It is deliberately a
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">separate
            binary</strong>
            from <code>emisar</code>. Your fleet hosts never carry publish code or publisher
            credentials. Only the workstation or CI job that builds the registry does. It shares
            the runner's pack loader. Published content hashes match
            <code>emisar pack validate</code>
            byte-for-byte.
          </p>
          <.docs_code
            phx-no-format
            label="shell"
            copy_text="go install github.com/andrewdryga/emisar/runner/cmd/packctl@latest"
          >go install github.com/andrewdryga/emisar/runner/cmd/packctl@latest</.docs_code>
        </li>

        <li id="build" class="scroll-mt-24 rounded-2xl border border-zinc-900 bg-zinc-950/60 p-6">
          <header class="flex items-center gap-3">
            <span class="grid h-8 w-8 shrink-0 place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 font-mono text-sm font-bold text-brand-300 ring-1 ring-brand-500/30">
              3
            </span>
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-zinc-50">Build the registry tree</h2>
          </header>
          <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <code>--base-url</code>
            is wherever you will host it. Tarball URLs in the catalog join onto it:
          </p>
          <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell">packctl catalog build --packs ./packs --out ./dist \
  --base-url https://packs.acme.internal</.docs_code>
          <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            The output is the whole registry. Immutable versioned or content-addressed artifacts,
            plus two mutable pointers:
          </p>
          <.docs_code phx-no-format>v1/catalog.json                                  latest catalog (mutable pointer)
v1/catalog/&lt;sha256&gt;.json                         immutable catalog snapshot
v1/suggest.json                                  lean suggest index (mutable pointer)
v1/schemas/*.vN.schema.json                      immutable versioned schemas
v1/packs/&lt;id&gt;/&lt;version&gt;/&lt;sha256&gt;/pack.tar.gz     immutable pack tarball</.docs_code>
        </li>

        <li id="host" class="scroll-mt-24 rounded-2xl border border-zinc-900 bg-zinc-950/60 p-6">
          <header class="flex items-center gap-3">
            <span class="grid h-8 w-8 shrink-0 place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 font-mono text-sm font-bold text-brand-300 ring-1 ring-brand-500/30">
              4
            </span>
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-zinc-50">Host it anywhere static</h2>
          </header>
          <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <code>packctl</code> can publish directly to GCS. It protects immutable object uploads
            with preconditions, so existing objects are never overwritten. It updates the mutable
            pointers last:
          </p>
          <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell">GOOGLE_OAUTH_ACCESS_TOKEN=$(gcloud auth print-access-token) \
  packctl catalog publish --dir ./dist --bucket acme-pack-registry</.docs_code>
          <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            For S3, MinIO, nginx, or another static host, sync the plain file tree with your
            preferred tool. Upload immutable objects first, then the two mutable pointers:
          </p>
          <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell">aws s3 sync ./dist s3://acme-pack-registry \
  --exclude "v1/catalog.json" --exclude "v1/suggest.json"
aws s3 cp ./dist/v1/suggest.json s3://acme-pack-registry/v1/suggest.json
aws s3 cp ./dist/v1/catalog.json s3://acme-pack-registry/v1/catalog.json</.docs_code>
        </li>

        <li
          id="install"
          class="scroll-mt-24 rounded-2xl border border-zinc-900 bg-zinc-950/60 p-6"
        >
          <header class="flex items-center gap-3">
            <span class="grid h-8 w-8 shrink-0 place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 font-mono text-sm font-bold text-brand-300 ring-1 ring-brand-500/30">
              5
            </span>
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-zinc-50">Install from it, fleet-wide</h2>
          </header>
          <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            Install the pack from its immutable tarball URL. Pin the hash from <code>catalog.json</code>. The runner rejects a different hash. Use the same URL and hash
            on every runner. To update the pack, install the new immutable URL.
          </p>
          <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell">sudo emisar pack install \
  https://packs.acme.internal/v1/packs/billing-tools/0.4.0/&lt;sha256&gt;/pack.tar.gz \
  --hash sha256:&lt;reviewed&gt;</.docs_code>
          <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            Trust the exact pack version and hash on the
            <a
              href={~p"/app/packs"}
              class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Packs</a>
            page. Actions use account trust, not the download host. If the portal reads this
            catalog, an exact pack, version, and hash match is trusted automatically. Any other new
            hash remains pending until an admin trusts it.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li
          id="publish"
          class="scroll-mt-24 rounded-2xl border border-zinc-900 bg-zinc-950/60 p-6"
        >
          <header class="flex items-center gap-3">
            <span class="grid h-8 w-8 shrink-0 place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 font-mono text-sm font-bold text-brand-300 ring-1 ring-brand-500/30">
              6
            </span>
            <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-zinc-50">Publish the next version safely</h2>
          </header>
          <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            Always build against the currently-published catalog so history carries forward.
            <code>--previous</code>
            enforces the registry's core guarantee. A pack whose files changed for an
            already-published <code>id@version</code>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">fails the build</strong>
            — bump the version instead. On a critical fix, republish and then
            revoke trust on the old version in each account's
            <a
              href={~p"/app/packs"}
              class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
            >Packs</a>
            page. The control plane then refuses new dispatches for that version. The runner
            separately checks the delivered expected hash against its installed files.
          </p>
          <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell">curl -fsS https://packs.acme.internal/v1/catalog.json -o current.json
packctl catalog build --packs ./packs --out ./dist \
  --base-url https://packs.acme.internal --previous current.json</.docs_code>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <%!-- What it does / does not change --%>
      <.docs_h2 id="tradeoffs">What your registry changes</.docs_h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 pl-5 text-base text-zinc-400">
        <li class="list-disc">
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">emisar works unchanged.</strong>
          Runners advertise installed packs regardless of source. Trust, policy, approvals, and
          audit apply identically to private packs.
        </li>
        <li class="list-disc">
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Air-gap friendly.</strong>
          Mirror the public registry tree or build one from a vendored <code>packs/</code>
          checkout. Host pack downloads inside your network. Hosted runners still connect outbound
          to the emisar control plane.
        </li>
        <li class="list-disc">
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Discovery follows two explicit settings.</strong>
          The control plane reads <code>EMISAR_PACK_CATALOG_URL</code>. For local suggestions, download
          <code>v1/suggest.json</code>
          and run <code>emisar pack suggest --catalog ./suggest.json</code>. A runner also advertises
          every installed pack to the portal.
        </li>
        <li class="list-disc">
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Use immutable URLs with this tree.</strong>
          The current name-based install and update commands use a different registry layout.
          <code>packctl</code>
          does not build that layout. Do not use <code>--registry</code>
          with this static tree.
        </li>
        <li class="list-disc">
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Revocation is trust revocation.</strong>
          Retire a version by revoking its trust in each account; the control plane then refuses
          dispatch for it. A <code>retired_below</code>
          floor applies only when the control plane reads <em>your</em>
          catalog — the hosted control plane reads the public catalog by default, so a private
          floor is authoring metadata until then. Hash pinning keeps the installed files you
          reviewed unchanged either way.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <%!-- Next steps --%>
      <div class="mt-16 flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3 border-t border-zinc-900 pt-8 text-sm">
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/publishing-packs"} class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">
          Author your own pack
        </.link>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-4">
          <.link navigate={~p"/docs/action-packs"} class="text-zinc-400 hover:text-zinc-200">
            Pack reference
          </.link>
          <.link navigate={~p"/packs"} class="text-zinc-400 hover:text-zinc-200">
            Browse the public registry
          </.link>
        </div>
      </div>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
